+ READINGS AND CORRECTIONS FOR ARM L (amendment, before start). Each item is a reading of a rule that this bar leaves open, or a correction of a fact. Four of them change how a rule of this bar is applied and are named here so that nobody reads the amendment as additions that leave the rules alone: R1 changes when the load rule (an INVALID rule and a HYGIENE rule) is tested and adds a void rule during the run, with limits relative to an idle baseline measured in the job; R2 changes how the clock-drop rule (HYGIENE and INVALID) is measured and what it is compared with; R4 changes a fixed sentence (the IMPLEMENTATION memory sentence) for this pair; R8 changes how the rotation rule is applied (a language-balanced cycle in place of a one-step rotation). The scope narrowing above also changes the headline sentence of rust-1. R3, R5, R6, R7 and R9 add records or procedure and move no rule. None of the nine items moves a boundary, a band, a statistic, a host role, a cap, an expected value or a label.
+ R1 Load. "uptime load average below 0.3 and fx status clear before and after" is read as: at the start of every arm run the 1-minute load average is below 0.3 and no other leg-* or sv5-* user unit is active and no llama-bench or ggml-rpc-server process is alive (the runner waits up to 10 minutes; if the host does not settle the rep is void and repeated); at the end of the run no other such unit is active; the load average at the end is recorded in every row and is not gated, because one single-thread arm run leaves the 1-minute load average near 1.0, so a gate on it would void every rep. In place of an idle test after the run, the runner measures the other load during the run itself: it reads the busy CPU time per logical CPU from /proc/stat (user, nice, system, irq, softirq and steal, in seconds) before the arm process starts and after it ends, and subtracts the arm process's own CPU time (utime plus stime of the reaped child) and the runner's own process CPU time. The two figures of a rep are (a) that remainder on the arm's CPU and on its hyper-threading sibling(s), together, as a fraction of the arm process's wall time, and (b) the same remainder over all logical CPUs of the host, in CPUs averaged over the arm process's wall time. The void limits are relative to an idle baseline measured in the job. After the idle gate has passed and before rep 0 (nothing timed yet, no arm running), the runner measures the baseline with the same foreign-load formula it uses for a rep: 6 windows of 20 s each. The baseline is the maximum over the 6 windows, separately for (a) and for (b). The limits are the baseline plus a margin, 2 percentage points for (a) and 0.05 CPU for (b), and never below 2 percent for (a) and 0.10 CPU for (b). A rep is void, recorded as VOID and repeated, when its figure (a) is above limit (a) or its figure (b) is above limit (b). If the baseline itself is above 15 percent for (a) or above 0.30 CPU for (b), the job ends INVALID with the reason host_not_idle and nothing is timed. The baseline (all 6 windows and the two maxima), the margins, the floors and the resulting limits are printed in the RESULT line and are carried in every row, next to the two figures of the rep; the margins, the floors and the baseline rule are fixed by this amendment, and no rep is excluded after the fact for any other load. Voids from this rule and from the idle test are counted together; more than 6 voids in one job stop the job as PARTIAL.
+ R2 Clock. "CPU MHz from /proc/cpuinfo before and after each rep, a rep with more than 15 percent drop is discarded" is read as: the reading is the MHz of the CPU the arm is pinned to (R6), taken from /proc/cpuinfo once a second from 2 s after the start of the arm process to its end. The reference is the median of the per-rep medians of the kept reps of that host so far (all arms); for the opening rep of a host it is that rep's own median. A rep is discarded and repeated when its lowest reading or its median is more than 15 percent below the reference, or when a thermal-throttle counter of the host changed during it (counters exist where the kernel exposes thermal_throttle in sysfs; the clock rule alone applies elsewhere). The third discard on a host makes the host INVALID, as the bar says. The reference is not the highest MHz seen anywhere in the job: on hosts with the schedutil governor and turbo (box2, box3) idle readings change by more than 15 percent within seconds, so such a reference would void the host by construction.
+ R3 Digests. For every build the host runs, the untimed --check witness of all 9 work rows (8 timed prompts and the warm-up row) is compared with REF_WITNESS.txt (the unpatched L-C output that made the 64-id file) before the timed reps and again after them; both must be equal. The idle gate and the baseline of R1 run before the before-check, so that the check does not run on a host that is not idle. The time of the after-check is reserved inside the cap test (R7).
+ R4 Loader (correction of fact). cis2_ref reads the weights file with std::fs::read into a heap buffer and verify3 reads it with fread into a heap buffer; neither arm maps the file. Wherever this bar says the Rust safetensors crate maps the weights file, or that load methods differ (mmap, read()), the result writer states instead: "both arms load the weights with read() into a heap buffer, widen to fp32 and free the buffer, so the transient 269 MB buffer is in both peaks". The sentence IMPLEMENTATION (memory) is written with that clause in place of "load methods differ (mmap, read())". The MIXED rule for peak PSS against peak anonymous bytes stays as written; its reason clause "because of the mmap against read() difference" is not written.
+ R5 CPU quota. The feeder sets CPUQuota to 100 percent times the number of logical CPUs on every job; cm-feed cannot leave the quota out (an empty value is refused by its field check and systemd rejects "infinity"). "no CPUQuota" is read as "no binding CPUQuota"; the runner logs cpu.max at every cgroup level, the result says whether a quota binds and states this reading as a disclosure.
+ R6 Pinning. The arm process is pinned to one CPU and the runner (the 50 ms sampler) to a CPU of a different physical core when the host has two or more (one shared CPU on box4); the same for both arms and for the untimed checks. The result states the CPUs.
+ R7 Cap accounting. The caps are lab totals per host. Every job writes its elapsed seconds into a file on the host; a later job on that host (including the single redo as 10 reps) gets the registered cap minus the sum of those, and never more. The cap test before each arm run counts the elapsed time, the longest run of that arm so far (or its check run before any timed run) and the reserved time of the after-check; an arm run that would not fit is not started and the arm is recorded COST-CAPPED and gets no label.
+ R8 Order. "arm order rotated each rep" is read as a language-balanced cycle of four orders, where rs-O2, rs-O3 are the two L-R builds and c-O2, c-O3 the two L-C builds of the host (gnu on box1 to box3, musl on box4 and box5): rep 0 rs-O2, c-O2, c-O3, rs-O3; rep 1 c-O3, rs-O3, rs-O2, c-O2; rep 2 rs-O3, c-O3, c-O2, rs-O2; rep 3 c-O2, rs-O2, rs-O3, c-O3; rep k uses the order of rep k mod 4. In every rep the two builds of one language sit at positions {0,3} or {1,2}, so the position sum of each language is 3 in every rep and over any number of reps each language holds the same mean position, 1.5 (0 = start of the order, 3 = end). Mean positions per build over 5 reps: rs-O2 1.2, rs-O3 1.8, c-O2 1.4, c-O3 1.6; over 10 reps: rs-O2 1.4, rs-O3 1.6, c-O2 1.6, c-O3 1.4. The builds of one language are not balanced against each other over 5 or 10 reps (differences of 0.2 to 0.6), the balance is between the languages. The result states the position of every build in every rep and the mean positions per build and per language. A discarded or void rep is repeated at the same position.
+ R9 Hosts. (a) The timing ruling of 2026-10-04 00:40Z (state/BOXES.md) is the reading of the RULE A paragraph for this bar: timing is allowed on an idle host with no binding CPUQuota and with load, MHz and throttle records per rep; box1 is the labelled host, box2 and box3 stay reported with no label, and the box1 cell of state/BOXES.md was edited on 2026-10-04 to cite that ruling. The opening box1 job is the host check that the RULE A paragraph asks for; its log is cited in the result. (b) The box1 job wrapper pauses cm-tick.timer and cm-rc-watch.timer (the periodic user units that would overlap reps) for the duration of the job and restores them on every exit path; RESULT prints timers_paused= and timers_restored=. It does not stop the feeder's own units (cm-legd.timer, cm-legd.service) or the job unit, and it does not stop a service that is already running. The idle gate (R1) refuses to start while any other leg or sv5 unit, a llama-bench process or a ggml-rpc-server process is alive.
